  "verbalization": "Tobramycin, a broad-spectrum antibiotic, is linked to Rosacea through a series of indirect pathways involving inflammatory conditions. The evidence suggests that Tobramycin may be associated with Rosacea via the induction of inflammatory responses, as it is indicated for conditions like Punctate Epithelial Keratoconjunctivitis and Iris Disease, which in turn are linked to Rosacea. The presence of Loteprednol Etabonate, a corticosteroid, in both Punctate Epithelial Keratoconjunctivitis and Iris Disease pathways further supports a role in modulating inflammation, which is a key mechanism in Rosacea pathogenesis. The integration of these pathways implies that Tobramycin may influence inflammatory processes, potentially contributing to the development or exacerbation of Rosacea through the activation of inflammatory mediators. The evidence, while not directly linking Tobramycin to Rosacea, provides a plausible mechanism through which it could be involved in the condition, emphasizing the importance of inflammatory regulation in the disease process.",
